course: dictionary
lesson: 3145
title: Embarrass and embarrassed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To embarrass someone means to make them feel shy, silly or uncomfortable in front of other people. Look at the words around it for clues: «If a classmate has no lunch, tell a teacher quietly so the school can help without embarrassing them.» The word quietly is the clue. Helping in private stops someone feeling uncomfortable in front of others.
explain: Notice «embarrassed». When you are embarrassed, you are the one feeling shy or uncomfortable. «A girl in Manchester had head lice but did not tell anyone because she was embarrassed.» She felt uncomfortable, so she kept it secret. The verb changes its ending: embarrass, embarrasses, embarrassed, embarrassing.
explain: Everyone gets embarrassed sometimes, like tripping in public or getting an answer wrong. Feeling embarrassed does not mean you did something bad. But we should never embarrass someone else on purpose. To check your meaning, swap "made them feel uncomfortable" into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Embarrass is a verb: you can embarrass someone, and then they feel embarrassed.
fact: «Embarrass» means to make someone feel shy, silly or uncomfortable in front of others.
fact: «Embarrass» is on the Years 5–6 spelling list in England, with words like «accommodate» and «necessary».
fact: Useful phrases: «embarrass someone», «be embarrassed about» and «embarrassed to say».
fact: Correcting someone in front of others can embarrass them, so it is kinder to do it quietly.
exercise: «If a classmate has no lunch, tell a teacher quietly so the school can help without embarrassing them.» What does embarrassing them mean? | *Making them feel uncomfortable in front of others | Helping them carry their lunch | Telling everyone their name | Giving them a prize
exercise: «A girl in Manchester had head lice but did not tell anyone because she was embarrassed.» Why did she keep it secret? | She was proud of it | *She felt shy and uncomfortable about it | She forgot what it was | She wanted to win a prize
exercise: Ali trips over in the playground and goes red in the face. What is he feeling? | Hungry | Thrilled | *Embarrassed | Bored
test: «Correcting someone in front of others can embarrass them, so it is kinder to do it quietly.» What is the kind choice? | *Tell them quietly, in private | Shout out their mistake in class | Share their mistake with everyone | Laugh at it loudly
test: Sara says, «I was embarrassed to say I had forgotten my book.» What does embarrassed mean here? | *Shy and uncomfortable about it | Very hungry | Extremely fast | Very angry
exam: Which word fits? «Please do not ___ your friend in front of the class.» | *embarrass | embarrassing | embarrassed | ancient
